# Phase 2 live probes against 192.168.6.14 proprietary-peer session
# Session plumbing works (confirmed finding 19); the question is whether
# nested-container slot-addressed writes make it past the chassis's OEM
# program's parser into actual endpoint I/O.

=== probe 1: flat 4-byte UpdateRequests ID=0x01..0x08 and 0x80/0x81/0xA0 ===
  11 TX, 0 RX responses - OEM parser accepts frames but no response

=== probe 2: broadcast IDs, finding-12 SwampE4 payload, longer-body UpdateRequests ===
  SwampE4 payload (03 0B 20 04 08 20 02 05 14 00 07 00 19) triggered one response
  16 TX longer-body UpdateRequests, 1 straggler response
  Both responses: 34 10 7c 03 39 34 / 34 10 7c 03 39 36 (serial-34 join 0x107c, ASCII '94'/'96')
  Pattern: byte-counter publication (same as IPID 1 S4221)

=== probe 3: nested-container UpdateRequests ===
  Chassis(0x01) alone, Chassis->Output(0x65), Chassis->Output->RMC chains
  Output IDs 0x01-0x08, 0x65-0x6C
  Chassis IDs 0x01-0x10
  2 responses, both identical format: 34 10 7c 03 '100' and 34 10 7c 03 '132'
  Still just the OEM byte-counter being published

=== probe 4: nested-container WRITES to RMC[?] ComPort[?] StringInput ===
  Full nested path Chassis(0x01)->Out(0x65 or 0x01)->RMC(0x01)->serial-15 leaf
  Join numbers: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 16, 17, 29, 100, 101
  Payload: 'PING\r' (5 bytes)
  20 TX total

  RESULTS:
    - 0 PING echoes received (TX->RX loopback on RMC never fired)
    - 2 counter responses: 34 10 7c 03 '142' and 34 10 7c 03 '172'
    - No disconnection, no error frames
    - Chassis silently parses writes but does NOT dispatch to slot I/O

=== 60s passive observation (no TX, after reconnect) ===
  Only heartbeats; zero unsolicited data frames
  Confirms the chassis does NOT publish state updates spontaneously -
  only publishes in response to packets we send (triggers counter bump)

=== INTERPRETATION ===
Session plumbing works (ALTERNATE_CONNECT, heartbeat, DATA frame
framing). The counter responses prove our DATA frames parse through
the chassis's receive stack. But the OEM program on the chassis does
NOT implement slot-addressed dispatch - it recognizes only its own
flat-join publish/counter pattern, same as all prior access methods.

Slot-addressed endpoint I/O requires either:
  (a) A user SIMPL program loaded on the chassis that defines
      DmRmc4kScalerC objects + handles slot-addressed TLDM traffic.
      The OEM default has neither.
  (b) An opcode/flag combination we have not yet discovered that
      unlocks slot-addressed dispatch in the OEM parser.

For this chassis + OEM program, we have reached the same ceiling as
findings 10, 16: the OEM default does not expose endpoint I/O via
ANY peer channel.
